    {
      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1186265/drawing-a-new-city-brasilia-fordlandia-and-ciudad-guayana",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1186265/drawing-a-new-city-brasilia-fordlandia-and-ciudad-guayana",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] Drawing a New City: Brasilia, Fordlandia, and Ciudad Guayana",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6abf/fa6e/27ac/8d01/89cb/b429/large_jpg/architecture-and-the-imagined-territories-of-latin-america_9.jpg?1790966396\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>A different social order is easier to imagine when it sits far from the one that already exists. In<a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1185977/latin-america-in-focus-this-month-in-architecture-inhabitation-memory-and-cultural-exchange\"> Latin America</a>, that distance has often been territorial. Parts of the region have repeatedly been treated as places where another world could begin. These places were never empty.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-06T07:30:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "ArchDaily",
          "url": "https://www.archdaily.com"
        }
      ],
      "image": "https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6abf/fa6e/27ac/8d01/89cb/b429/large_jpg/architecture-and-the-imagined-territories-of-latin-america_9.jpg?1790966396",
      "_score": 45,
      "_quality": 0,
      "_relevance": 0,
      "_local_score": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185518/architecture-as-translation-africanfuturism-in-folklore-film-and-speculative-art",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185518/architecture-as-translation-africanfuturism-in-folklore-film-and-speculative-art",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] Architecture as Translation: Africanfuturism in Folklore, Film, and Speculative Art",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6abd/5a27/fdbd/f801/8857/ed8f/large_jpg/architecture-as-translation-for-the-africanfuturist-present-heres-and-elsewheres_10.jpg?1790794284\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>\"Take your dream suppressants.\" The opening line in Wanuri Kahiū's <a href=\"https://vimeo.com/46891859?utm_medium=website&amp;utm_source=archdaily.com\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Pumzi</em></a>, directed at our protagonist Asha, enforces the order <a href=\"https://www.scielo.org.za/scielo.php?pid=S0041-476X2025000100004&amp;script=sci_arttext&amp;utm_medium=website&amp;utm_source=archdaily.com\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">of severing ties with hopeful futures and ancestral memories in pasts shared</a> to compel her focus on the perpetual present in the Maitu Community's <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1185278/architectural-heterotopias-temporary-installations-as-alternative-worlds\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">heterotopia</a> of an enclave. The bird's-eye view shot of the Maitu reveals a sterile, drone-like structure with a panoptic sphere at its center where normal human behavior is medicalized<a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1185010/escaping-systems-architecture-beyond-familiar-ground\">. The layout is built around closed-loop recycling systems</a> as apt as a self-sufficient organism, designed to escape the toxic environment of a sickly Earth, where every pipe and corridor is engineered to harvest human excretion for water generation, thereby absorbing the roles of nature and state both. The community's architecture perfectly translates the film's message with its physicality.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-06T04:00:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "ArchDaily",
          "url": "https://www.archdaily.com"
        }
      ],
      "image": "https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6abd/5a27/fdbd/f801/8857/ed8f/large_jpg/architecture-as-translation-for-the-africanfuturist-present-heres-and-elsewheres_10.jpg?1790794284",
      "_score": 44,
      "_quality": 0,
      "_relevance": 0,
      "_local_score": 0
    },
